th the  regions that are available secondly if  you're uploading a lot of small files  you may not know this but every object  storage out there has some sort of  latency or some sort of small overhead  that happens when you actually push a  file to the the ending destination or  the ending bucket so being able to push  let's say 500 small files or of 125  kilobytes in size actually gets  associated with a large amount of  unnecessary overhead so if you consider  GS utils multi-threaded option the juice  util - M option you can actually multi  thread all of these smaller files into  one big push to GCS and as you can see  on the graph on the left we did a test  where we actually saw for two over four  times the amount of throughput just by  using this - Emma  so definitely something to consider if  you have a bunch of small files now  conversely if you have maybe some really  large files so you're doing some video  uploads some multimedia uploads maybe  some really large log files or something  like that there's another option of  actually breaking one large object into  multiple smaller objects and it's called  composite parallel uploads so we have  this another gsutil option where  essentially when you perform an upload  of let's say one massive file so on the  left you can see a 500 megabyte file we  actually split that up into multiple  chunks upload those in parallel and then  once they all arrive in GCS GCS actually  combines them together and then performs  a checksum to make sure that everything  got compiled successfully and as you can  see on the left-hand side we see quite a  bit of a performance improvement by  using this option compared to the  default option yeah now one thing to be  aware of though is that you should not  be using this for near line or cold line  storage the reason why pretty simple is  that when you upload all these small  little chunks and then it combines those  chunks into one big chunk it actually  deletes those individual chunks